※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4598
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4599 LANG_PLACE_NOT_WRITABLE

※ The environment is read-only, and that is a property of its type.
※
※ `⸨str: str⸩` says a table may be read and `mut ⸨str: str⸩` says entries may be
※ put in it, the way `&T` and `&mut T` say it of a place.  A collection is a
※ handle -- the one who made it and the one who was handed it reach one table --
※ so what may be done to that table belongs to the type rather than to any one
※ name for it.  `⎕environ` is written without the `mut`, so:
※
※ - an entry assignment is refused (4598), and
※ - the name is not a place to put a different dictionary either (4599), since
※   what stands there is what every part of the program reads.
※
※ So the dictionary says what the process started with and goes on saying it.
※ Nothing in the language changes the process's own environment either; there is
※ no way to start a process, so an environment a program could change would be a
※ change nobody could observe.
※
※ Compare: **Rust**, where `std::env::set_var` exists and is unsafe; **Go**, with
※ `os.Setenv`, which changes the process; **C**, with `putenv` and `setenv`;
※ **Python**, where `os.environ` is a writable mapping that writes through.  Each
※ of those changes what the process passes on to what it starts.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕environ⸨"PL4G_TEST"⸩ ← "no"
    ⎕environ ← ⸨"PL4G_TEST": "no"⸩
    0u6
